                [
                    91.0,
                    879.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "We learn by connecting new information to what we already know. In sentences, readers therefore prefer to encounter information that is old or familiar to them before they encounter information that is new or unfamiliar. So:",
            "block_bbox": [
                91,
                886,
                783,
                963
            ],
            "block_id": 11,
            "block_order": 10,
            "group_id": 11,
            "global_block_id": 11,
            "global_group_id": 11,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    91.0,
                    886.0
                ],
                [
                    783.0,
                    886.0
                ],
                [
                    783.0,
                    963.0
                ],
                [
                    91.0,
                    963.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "1. Begin sentences with information familiar to your readers. Readers get that familiar information in two ways. First, they remember words from the sentences they just read. That's why in our example about black holes, the beginning of 2b coheres with the end of 1 and why the beginning of 3 coheres with the end of 2b. Second, readers bring to a sentence a general knowledge of its subject. We would not be surprised, for example, to find the next sentence 4 begin like this:",
            "block_bbox": [
                92,
                975,
                785,
                1156
            ],
            "block_id": 12,
            "block_order": 11,
            "group_id": 12,
            "global_block_id": 12,
